1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,718
Welcome to our deep dive into artificial neural networks.

2
00:00:03,718 --> 00:00:08,262
If you use a smartphone, stream movies, or interact with digital

3
00:00:08,262 --> 00:00:12,393
assistants, you are already using technology powered by neural networks.

4
00:00:12,393 --> 00:00:16,524
But how do these systems actually work beneath the surface?

5
00:00:16,524 --> 00:00:21,068
In this lesson, we are going to demystify artificial neural networks.

6
00:00:21,068 --> 00:00:25,199
We will explore their biological origins, look inside their architecture

7
00:00:25,199 --> 00:00:29,743
to understand how data moves through them, and finally, discover how

8
00:00:29,743 --> 00:00:33,874
these computational models learn from their mistakes to make remarkably

9
00:00:33,874 --> 00:00:34,700
accurate predictions.

10
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:39,544
To understand artificial neural networks, it helps to look at where the

11
00:00:39,544 --> 00:00:41,965
idea came from: our own brains.

12
00:00:41,965 --> 00:00:46,809
The human brain is made up of roughly eighty-six billion neurons, which

13
00:00:46,809 --> 00:00:50,845
are specialized cells that communicate with one another using electrical

14
00:00:50,845 --> 00:00:52,056
and chemical signals.

15
00:00:52,056 --> 00:00:55,689
Each neuron receives signals from other neurons, processes that

16
00:00:55,689 --> 00:01:00,533
information, and if the signal is strong enough, fires an output signal

17
00:01:00,533 --> 00:01:02,551
to pass the message along.

18
00:01:02,551 --> 00:01:06,184
Computer scientists in the mid-twentieth century looked at this

19
00:01:06,184 --> 00:01:10,624
biological marvel and wondered if they could replicate it in software.

20
00:01:10,624 --> 00:01:14,660
They created the artificial neuron, a mathematical model designed to

21
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:15,871
mimic biological processing.

22
00:01:15,871 --> 00:01:20,311
While our artificial models are vastly simpler than the human brain,

23
00:01:20,311 --> 00:01:23,944
they share the same foundational concept: interconnected units working

24
00:01:23,944 --> 00:01:27,576
together to process complex information, recognize patterns, and make

25
00:01:27,576 --> 00:01:27,980
decisions.

26
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:31,406
An artificial neural network is constructed from layers of artificial

27
00:01:31,406 --> 00:01:33,462
neurons, which we often call nodes.

28
00:01:33,462 --> 00:01:38,601
At a minimum, a network has an input layer to receive data, one or more

29
00:01:38,601 --> 00:01:43,055
hidden layers to process it, and an output layer that delivers the final

30
00:01:43,055 --> 00:01:43,397
result.

31
00:01:43,397 --> 00:01:47,166
Within these layers, every connection between nodes is governed by two

32
00:01:47,166 --> 00:01:48,879
crucial components: weights and biases.

33
00:01:48,879 --> 00:01:51,962
Think of a weight as a measure of importance.

34
00:01:51,962 --> 00:01:55,731
If a particular input is very influential in determining the outcome,

35
00:01:55,731 --> 00:02:00,185
the connection leading from it will have a high weight; if it is

36
00:02:00,185 --> 00:02:02,925
irrelevant, the weight will be close to zero.

37
00:02:02,925 --> 00:02:06,694
The bias, on the other hand, acts like an adjustable threshold.

38
00:02:06,694 --> 00:02:11,833
It determines how easy or difficult it is for a node to fire its signal,

39
00:02:11,833 --> 00:02:15,944
allowing the network to shift its activation patterns to better fit the

40
00:02:15,944 --> 00:02:18,000
data it is trying to learn.

41
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,074
Data moves through a neural network in a process called forward

42
00:02:22,074 --> 00:02:22,444
propagation.

43
00:02:22,444 --> 00:02:25,777
Imagine you want a network to recognize handwritten digits.

44
00:02:25,777 --> 00:02:30,221
You feed an image of a handwritten number into the input layer.

45
00:02:30,221 --> 00:02:35,035
Each pixel value is passed to the nodes in the first hidden layer.

46
00:02:35,035 --> 00:02:38,739
Inside each node, a mathematical operation takes place: it multiplies

47
00:02:38,739 --> 00:02:42,812
every incoming input value by its corresponding weight, adds them all

48
00:02:42,812 --> 00:02:45,405
together, and then adds the bias value.

49
00:02:45,405 --> 00:02:49,478
The resulting sum is a single number that represents the preliminary

50
00:02:49,478 --> 00:02:50,959
finding of that node.

51
00:02:50,959 --> 00:02:54,663
This number is then passed forward to the next layer.

52
00:02:54,663 --> 00:02:59,107
This cascade continues step by step until it reaches the output layer,

53
00:02:59,107 --> 00:03:03,180
where the network produces its final prediction, such as guessing that

54
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:05,402
the image is the number seven.

55
00:03:05,402 --> 00:03:09,476
This initial prediction is purely based on the current weights and

56
00:03:09,476 --> 00:03:13,179
biases, meaning it might be wildly incorrect before the network

57
00:03:13,179 --> 00:03:13,920
undergoes training.

58
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:18,147
If a neural network only performed basic multiplication and addition

59
00:03:18,147 --> 00:03:23,219
across its layers, it would just be a series of linear equations.

60
00:03:23,219 --> 00:03:27,445
Linear systems are limited; they cannot capture complex patterns, like

61
00:03:27,445 --> 00:03:30,826
distinguishing a cat's face from a car's grille.

62
00:03:30,826 --> 00:03:33,785
To solve this, we introduce activation functions.

63
00:03:33,785 --> 00:03:38,434
An activation function sits inside each node and decides whether the

64
00:03:38,434 --> 00:03:43,506
node's computed sum is important enough to pass along, and in what

65
00:03:43,506 --> 00:03:43,929
format.

66
00:03:43,929 --> 00:03:47,733
By applying a non-linear transformation to the data, activation

67
00:03:47,733 --> 00:03:51,537
functions allow the network to learn intricate, curved boundaries

68
00:03:51,537 --> 00:03:52,805
between different categories.

69
00:03:52,805 --> 00:03:56,609
Common activation functions include the Sigmoid function, which squashes

70
00:03:56,609 --> 00:04:01,258
outputs between zero and one, and ReLU, which stands for Rectified

71
00:04:01,258 --> 00:04:05,062
Linear Unit, passing positive values straight through while turning

72
00:04:05,062 --> 00:04:06,753
negative values into zero.

73
00:04:06,753 --> 00:04:10,557
These mathematical functions give neural networks the flexibility to

74
00:04:10,557 --> 00:04:12,670
solve highly complex real-world problems.

75
00:04:12,670 --> 00:04:16,566
When a neural network makes a prediction during forward propagation, how

76
00:04:16,566 --> 00:04:19,754
do we know if it got the answer right?

77
00:04:19,754 --> 00:04:21,525
We use a loss function.

78
00:04:21,525 --> 00:04:25,421
A loss function is a mathematical formula that compares the network's

79
00:04:25,421 --> 00:04:27,546
prediction against the true, correct answer.

80
00:04:27,546 --> 00:04:32,859
If the network predicts that a picture of a dog is a cat, the loss

81
00:04:32,859 --> 00:04:36,046
function calculates a high penalty, representing a large error.

82
00:04:36,046 --> 00:04:40,651
If the prediction is very close to the truth, the penalty is small.

83
00:04:40,651 --> 00:04:44,901
When we calculate this error across an entire dataset of many examples,

84
00:04:44,901 --> 00:04:47,735
we get what is called the cost function.

85
00:04:47,735 --> 00:04:51,277
The cost function provides a single score representing the overall

86
00:04:51,277 --> 00:04:52,693
performance of the model.

87
00:04:52,693 --> 00:04:57,298
A high cost means the model is performing poorly, while a low cost

88
00:04:57,298 --> 00:04:58,360
indicates high accuracy.

89
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:03,319
The primary goal of training a neural network is to drive this cost as

90
00:05:03,319 --> 00:05:05,090
close to zero as possible.

91
00:05:05,090 --> 00:05:09,213
Reducing the cost requires a feedback mechanism, which is where

92
00:05:09,213 --> 00:05:12,100
backpropagation and gradient descent come into play.

93
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:16,636
Once the loss function calculates the error at the output layer,

94
00:05:16,636 --> 00:05:20,759
backpropagation travels backward through the network, from the output to

95
00:05:20,759 --> 00:05:21,584
the input.

96
00:05:21,584 --> 00:05:26,944
It uses calculus to figure out exactly how much each weight and bias

97
00:05:26,944 --> 00:05:29,006
contributed to the final error.

98
00:05:29,006 --> 00:05:32,717
Armed with this blame-assignment information, the network applies an

99
00:05:32,717 --> 00:05:34,779
optimization algorithm called gradient descent.

100
00:05:34,779 --> 00:05:39,727
Gradient descent tweaks the weights and biases by tiny amounts in the

101
00:05:39,727 --> 00:05:41,789
direction that reduces the error.

102
00:05:41,789 --> 00:05:45,500
By repeating this cycle of forward propagation, error calculation,

103
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:49,211
backpropagation, and weight adjustment across thousands or millions of

104
00:05:49,211 --> 00:05:51,685
examples, the network gradually tunes itself.

105
00:05:51,685 --> 00:05:56,633
It learns from its mistakes, transforming from a random guesser into a

106
00:05:56,633 --> 00:05:57,870
highly accurate predictor.

107
00:05:57,870 --> 00:06:02,564
In summary, artificial neural networks mimic biological systems to

108
00:06:02,564 --> 00:06:07,779
process data, learn from experience, and make complex decisions through

109
00:06:07,779 --> 00:06:10,908
structured layers, mathematical adjustments, and continuous

110
00:06:10,908 --> 00:06:11,430
optimization.
